# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Their Own Words v2 content — Act V. See _tow_content_a.py for entry format."""

ACT_5 = [

dict(qid='a5-camp-1', act=5, theme='camp', source=('cand', 62), recip='to Alexander', date_str='January 11, 1865',
  stem='Frances’s own village now wakes to reveille. Why were there soldiers garrisoning a New York border town in 1865?',
  choices=['Confederate raiders had struck across the Canadian border months earlier', 'The town was a mustering-out camp', 'Draft riots had spread to the countryside', 'They were guarding against a British invasion'],
  answer=0,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. In October 1864 Confederate agents rode out of Canada, robbed the banks of St. Albans, Vermont — twenty-odd miles from Champlain — and fled back across the line. The border militarized overnight.",
  whyWrong={'1': "Champlain's turn as a rendezvous comes later — Alexander actually musters out at Ogdensburgh. In January the blue uniforms were sentries, not returnees.",
            '3': "Anglo-American tension was real (the raiders sheltered in Canada), but the garrison watched for raiders, not redcoats."},
  expansion="The St. Albans Raid — October 19, 1864, the war's northernmost action — was twenty-two Confederate agents out of Canada robbing three Vermont banks of $200,000, killing a citizen, and recrossing the line to a Canadian court that let most of them go. The raid was small; its echo was not. It confirmed the North's standing fear of a hostile sanctuary on its border (Confederate operations out of Canada had also targeted Lake Erie shipping and, that November, tried to burn New York's hotels), strained relations with Britain to the edge of crisis, and pulled troops from the war to picket a 400-mile frontier of farm towns. For border communities like Champlain — one ride from the line — the war's last winter thus arrived in person: drill in the streets, reveille and tattoo bracketing the day, passes and provosts among the kitchens that had spent four years only READING about garrisons. The war ran out of front just as it reached her window.",
  context="Four years of Frances's letters describe the war as an absence — sons gone, mails watched, casualty lists read at the post office. This is the only one where the war is a PRESENCE: 'Our streets are blue with soldiers.' There's something almost wry in her cataloguing the army's day-rhythm (reveille, tattoo) with the competence of a woman who has been managing soldiers by mail since 1861 — she knows the vocabulary better than the garrison's recruits do. The reversal underneath is what gives the sentence its charge: for once, SHE writes the camp letter, to a son who knows the sounds by heart. The same letter (it appears twice more in this act) carries her hope discipline about the war's end and her relief that Alexander is 'among the living' — the full register of the home front's last winter, in one envelope from a militarized village."),

dict(qid='a5-body-1', act=5, theme='body', source=('cand', 63), recip='to Alexander', date_str='January 11, 1865',
  stem='Frances had already counted Alexander among the dead — wrongly. What does her sentence capture about the war’s toll?',
  choices=['How often the army misreported casualties', 'The psychological weight carried by families and soldiers — a toll no ledger counted', 'That mail was faster than casualty lists', 'That soldiers hid their wounds from home'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Between a battle and a letter stood days or weeks in which families simply did not know — and lived every outcome at once. The era had no word for what that did to people; we do.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Misreporting happened — but Alexander hadn't been reported anything. SILENCE did this, not error.",
            '3': "The brothers did soften things for her — but her fear here needed no wound to feed on, only an empty mailbox."},
  expansion="Read her grammar closely: 'my fears had NUMBERED you with the dead' — grief run in advance, on the strength of an empty mailbox during Sherman's off-the-map march. Modern psychology has names for the home front's condition — anticipatory grief, ambiguous loss — but the 1860s had only endurance and Providence, and no framework at all for what four years of managed dread did to mothers, wives, and the soldiers who felt their families' fear pressing back through the mails. The Civil War generation carried its psychological casualties uncounted: 'soldier's heart' was the closest the era came to a diagnosis for the men; for the women, there wasn't even that. Historians now read letters like Frances's as the primary evidence of the war's invisible ledger — the costs that never reached a muster roll. This archive's seventeen letters from her constitute exactly such a record: one woman's four-year experiment in hoping strategically, documented in her own hand.",
  context="This is the last movement of a fear the archive lets you watch across three years: 1862, the post-office mornings for Henry; 1863, the imagined Libby Prison and field hospitals; now 1865, mourning-by-arithmetic during a five-week silence — and then the letter that ends it, answered with this trembling relief. What's new this time is the confession's tense: she tells Alexander AFTERWARD what she had let herself believe, which she never quite did in the earlier crises. The war taught her its worst lesson in 1862 — that the unthinkable arrives by ordinary mail — and every later silence replayed it; but it also taught her to keep writing into the dark, and the archive is the proof. When people ask why a mother's letters belong beside battle accounts in a war archive, this sentence is the answer in nine words."),

dict(qid='a5-purse-1', act=5, theme='purse', source=('cand', 36), recip='to his sister Fannie', date_str='June 27, 1865',
  stem='The war is won, but Alexander is still in camp doing paperwork. What did it take to send a million-man army home?',
  choices=['Each soldier simply left when his term expired', 'Muster-out rolls, final pay settlements, and transportation — an administrative campaign of its own', 'A year of compulsory occupation duty for all veterans', 'Soldiers paid their own way home and were reimbursed later'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “Make out our papers” — every man's account settled: pay owed, bounty due, clothing overdrawn, then rail and steamer home to a state rendezvous for discharge. The Union demobilized some 800,000 men in about six months.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Terms had expired for thousands who still waited — no man left without his discharge in hand unless he wanted 'deserter' on the rolls forever.",
            '3': "Transportation home was the government's bill — that's the 'get transportation' he's waiting on."},
  expansion="The Union's last campaign was clerical. Every soldier's muster-out required a completed roll — five copies, error-free, reconciling three-plus years of pay, bounty installments, clothing allowances drawn against, hospital time, and detached service — before the paymaster would touch him. Regiments then traveled as units to their home-state rendezvous (Alexander's was Ogdensburgh) for final payment and discharge, so the railroads spent the summer of 1865 running the war in reverse. It worked at astonishing speed: roughly 800,000 men mustered out between May and November, the great volunteer army dissolving back into farms and shops within a year, its final ledgers accurate to the cent — arguably the young republic's most underrated administrative feat. The cultural fact rode beside the fiscal one: the United States chose, deliberately, NOT to keep a mass army in peacetime. The men wanted precisely what Alexander wants in this sentence: papers, pay, and the road home.",
  context="Alexander wrote this letter — his grand valediction, quoted twice in this act — from the same camps around Alexandria where the Grand Review had passed a month before, and its housekeeping sentences are as revealing as its oratory: after four years, Antietam to Atlanta to the sea, the war's actual ending is a queue for paperwork. He tells Fannie the mechanism precisely (papers → transportation → Ogdensburgh → 'discharged and paid off') because the family's planning needs the sequence — when to expect him, what money arrives with him. Ogdensburgh closes his circle exactly: it's where the 60th New York organized in 1861, when he was a seventeen-year-old assuring his mother he hadn't enlisted for novelty. The archive's last letters are full of this geometry — the war returning everyone to their starting coordinates, minus the ones it kept."),

dict(qid='a5-nation-1', act=5, theme='nation', source=('cand', 35), recip='to his sister Fannie', date_str='June 27, 1865',
  stem='A sergeant sums up what four years bought. In his words “free and united,” what two outcomes of the war is he naming?',
  choices=['Free trade and united markets', 'Emancipation and the preservation of the Union', 'Freedom of the press and a united party system', 'Free land in the West and a united currency'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “Free” — slavery destroyed, the Thirteenth Amendment through Congress and ratifying that very year. “United” — secession broken. A farm-boy sergeant fused the war's two great results into six words.",
  whyWrong={'0': "The economy transformed — greenbacks, railroads, tariffs — but that's not the register he's writing in. 'In point of freedom, no equal' is about human beings.",
            '3': "Homesteads and greenbacks were real wartime creations — but read his sentence again: honor and freedom are its stakes, not acreage."},
  expansion="The order of Alexander's adjectives is a historical finding in itself. In 1861, Union soldiers' letters overwhelmingly named one war aim: the Union — the Revolution's inheritance, 'the last best hope.' Emancipation entered the ranks' vocabulary gradually and unevenly: through the logic of war (every enslaved laborer freed weakened the Confederacy), through what soldiers saw marching south, through Black regiments' fighting record after 1863. Historians who track this evolution across thousands of letters call it the great measurable shift in the war generation's mind — and here it stands completed in one sentence from a farm-boy sergeant: 'free' FIRST, 'united' second, unprompted, to his sister. Not every veteran made that journey (this same archive holds letters that didn't), which makes the specimens that did the more valuable. The 272 words of the Gettysburg Address made the argument; sentences like this one, multiplied by a hundred thousand hands, are the evidence it landed.",
  context="Here is the full arc this game has been walking you along: January 1862, a seventeen-year-old defends his enlistment as more than novelty; June 1865, the same hand writes a valediction — 'four years of unparalleled struggling… a free and united nation… in point of freedom, no equal.' The rest of the letter (one spoke away) earns the big sentence with particulars: 'Treason walked our legislative halls,' he writes, remembering 1861's chaos, and reckons what the recovery cost. He was twenty-one when he wrote this — an age when his modern counterparts declare majors — with Antietam, Gettysburg, Lookout Mountain, Atlanta, and the March to the Sea behind him. If you keep one sentence from this archive as the answer to 'what did the soldiers think it was for,' the family's own answer is this one. And notice, one last time, WHO he's telling: Fannie. The Hubbells did their deepest thinking to each other."),

dict(qid='a5-family-1', act=5, theme='family',
  source=('slice', 'LTR-1865-07-27-001', 'We had a grand reception.', 'at the tea table.'),
  recip='to his mother — his last letter of the war', date_str='July 27, 1865',
  stem='This is the final wartime letter in the whole collection. Why do letter archives like this one end here?',
  choices=['Paper shortages after the war made writing expensive', 'Letters exist because of distance — when the family was reunited, the record fell silent', 'The army censored veterans’ correspondence', 'The family stopped keeping letters after the war'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “I will tell you all about it at the tea table” — the collection's last wartime sentence promises a story that would never need to be written down. The archive ends because the distance did.",
  whyWrong={'3': "They kept everything — that's why you're here. The family preserved and eventually transcribed the whole collection across five generations.",
            '0': "Paper was cheap again by 1865 — and he had stationery in hand (borrowed, he admits, from 'one of the boys'). What he no longer had was the need."},
  expansion="This is a lesson about sources themselves — the historian's craft of asking not just what a document says but why it exists. A letter collection is a map of separations: it begins when distance opens and dies the moment distance closes, which means the richest personal archives of the nineteenth century are byproducts of its disruptions — wars, migrations, gold rushes. It also means the record is structurally silent exactly where life is most whole: no letters describe the reunion, the tea table, the decades of ordinary proximity that followed. Historians call this a survivorship problem of a special kind — not lost evidence, but evidence never created. What the Hubbells' postwar lives left instead were the state's dry traces: pension files, census lines, cemetery stones — including the ones that let this project correct the family's own memory of a death date 160 years on. The letters end; the reckoning of them, which you are inside of right now, does not.",
  context="Every ending in this letter is doubled. Alexander writes from Camp Wheeler, Ogdensburgh — the ground where his regiment formed in 1861 — waiting for the last paymaster, promising the story of the 'grand reception' in person: the archive's four-year voice, signing off mid-anecdote, forever. What the tea table never got to hear: James, discharged from his own regiment, died that October on the journey home from Savannah — the war outliving its armistice by one brother. Charles carried his wounds another decade, to 1875. Frances kept every page. And so the promise in this sentence was kept in a way Alexander couldn't have meant: he DID tell it all — to us, in the 272 letters a mother saved because a seventeen-year-old once wrote 'write often, write often, often.' You've now heard the whole conversation. The finale will show you what you learned from it — and the archive's doors stay open."),

dict(qid='a5-camp-2', act=5, theme='camp', source=('gap', 'V-camp', 0), recip='to his mother', date_str='June 16, 1865',
  stem='“Our corps and division has been broken up. Gen. Geary has issued his farewell order and gone.” What is Alexander describing?',
  choices=['A disciplinary dissolution after a mutiny', 'The army itself being dismantled — the units that were a soldier’s world dissolving before the men went home', 'A reorganization for the invasion of Texas', 'His transfer to another corps'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. Demobilization ran top-down: corps and divisions dissolved by order, generals published farewells and departed, and the men lingered in camps that were suddenly just fields with tents.",
  whyWrong={'2': "A force WAS sent toward Texas in 1865 (watching the French in Mexico) — but Alexander's corps wasn't in it; his war was dissolving, not redeploying.",
            '3': "No transfer — there was decreasingly anything to transfer TO. The address book of the army was being erased line by line."},
  expansion="A Civil War soldier's country, day to day, was his regiment, division, and corps — badges worn on caps, flags fought under, generals whose names organized memory ('we were Geary's men'). Demobilization unmade that world in weeks: the volunteer corps were discontinued through the summer of 1865, each dissolution marked by a printed farewell order — a genre of its own, equal parts commendation and elegy — read to the ranks and pasted into diaries. John White Geary, the huge Pennsylvanian who had commanded Alexander's division from Lookout Mountain to Savannah (and had been Kansas's governor before the war, and would be Pennsylvania's after), issued his and left like the rest. The men's letters from these weeks record a strange double emotion historians have noted ever since: desperate to go home, and unexpectedly bereft as the institution that had held their whole adult lives so far was struck like a tent. Veterans' organizations — the Grand Army of the Republic above all — would spend fifty years rebuilding on paper what orders like Geary's dissolved.",
  context="Alexander delivers the news in the flat voice he reserves for things too large to editorialize: corps broken up, farewell order issued, 'and gone.' He had followed Geary up Lookout Mountain and across Georgia; 'Geary's division' is half his identity, and it ceased to exist by circular. The same June 16 letter (it appears three times in this act — the richest single letter of the war's end) carries the sold watch, the hunger, and the first word of going home: dissolution, privation, and homecoming in one envelope. Worth holding beside the tea-table letter six weeks later: between the army ending and home beginning stretched this strange in-between season — units gone, pay pending, men camping inside the shell of an institution — and Alexander's letters are among the archive's best evidence of what that felt like from inside."),

dict(qid='a5-body-2', act=5, theme='body', source=('gap', 'V-body', 0), recip='to his mother', date_str='June 16, 1865',
  stem='Weeks after the war’s end, Alexander mentions — almost in passing — that he sold his watch “to buy bread to keep life in me.” What does this reveal about the war’s final months?',
  choices=['He had gambled away his pay', 'Even the victorious army’s supply could collapse — soldiers on the great marches went genuinely hungry', 'Camp sutlers refused paper money in 1865', 'He was saving his rations to sell'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. On the Carolinas march the columns outran their wagons for weeks at a stretch — and a veteran sergeant quietly liquidated his pocket watch for bread. Victory and hunger shared the road north.",
  whyWrong={'0': "Nothing in four years of his letters suggests it — and he names the purchase: bread. This is a supply story, not a vice story.",
            '2': "Sutlers took greenbacks greedily — when sutlers could be FOUND, which on the march through the Carolinas they could not."},
  expansion="Sherman's armies marched from Savannah through the Carolinas in early 1865 — a harder campaign than Georgia: winter rains, swamps corduroyed mile by mile, and a countryside deliberately poorer than Georgia's plantation belt. Foraging by order fed the columns unevenly at best; by the campaign's Virginia end, even sergeants were trading personal property for food. The detail belongs to a larger truth the victory parades obscured: the Union's logistical miracle was real in aggregate and ragged in the particular — the same army could express $350 home (Alexander does, in this very letter) and have starved weeks earlier, because pay, rations, and campaigns ran on separate clocks. A soldier's watch was typically his single most valuable possession, often a gift of home. 'To keep life in me' prices the campaign exactly: everything above survival was negotiable. Frances's January ache — 'how I do wish I could send you a box of good things,' written when he first mentioned hunger — is this question's home-front mirror, three letters away in the same archive.",
  context="The construction is pure Alexander: 'PERHAPS I did not tell you' — four years of managing his mother's fear distilled into three words that mean 'I definitely did not tell you, because you would have suffered over it, and it's safe to say now that I'm nearly home.' The disclosure schedule of soldiers' letters is a study in itself: hardships surface only once survived, wounds minimized in real time and detailed in retrospect (James did precisely the same after Cedar Creek). Notice what he sold, too — a WATCH, the token that measured the distances this family lived by. And notice the sequence in his June letters: hunger confessed, $350 expressed home, watch mourned lightly, homecoming promised. The account of the war closes the way it ran: hardship converted into narrative, money flowing home, and the mother's imagination protected until the facts could do no harm."),

dict(qid='a5-purse-2', act=5, theme='purse', source=('gap', 'V-purse', 0), recip='to his mother', date_str='July 27, 1865',
  stem='Home state reached, war over — and Alexander is stuck in camp because one official hasn’t arrived. Who is he waiting for?',
  choices=['The mustering officer with his discharge papers', 'The paymaster — no final payment, no going home', 'The railroad agent with transportation vouchers', 'The regimental chaplain for the farewell service'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “Waiting for the Paymaster ever since” — the war's last queue. A regiment at its home rendezvous could do nothing but drill, write letters, and watch the road for the man with the iron safe.",
  whyWrong={'0': "The muster-out rolls were done — that bureaucratic mountain was climbed at Alexandria (his June letter walks you through it). Only the money was missing.",
            '2': "Transportation had delivered them — Virginia to Ogdensburgh. The government moved the men faster than it moved their wages, to no soldier's surprise."},
  expansion="The final settlement was the one appointment no volunteer would leave without: months of arrears, the withheld bounty balance, allowances — for many men well over a hundred dollars, the largest sum they had ever held. Paymasters crisscrossed the northern states all summer with escorts and iron safes, perpetually behind schedule, while regiments idled at rendezvous camps in a purgatory every soldier's letter of July 1865 describes identically: war over, home visible, money late. (The report that some of the war's very last camps ran on borrowed stationery and patience is not apocryphal — Alexander's own letter is written on paper begged from 'one of the boys.') When the paymaster finally came, the man signed, pocketed the war's conclusion in greenbacks, and walked out a civilian. The Hubbell collection's financial thread — $8 in 1861, sutler discipline, greenbacks, arrears, bounties, a dead brother's back pay — ends here, at a pay table in Ogdensburgh, one signature from closed.",
  context="Twenty miles from his mother's kitchen and pinned in camp by an absent accountant — the shape of the whole war's purse, one last time: the government's promises always arrived, and always late, and the family planned around the lag to the end ('I have some doubts whether I shall be able to be home this week'). This is the same letter as the tea table — the collection's final wartime page — and it's fitting that its two surviving sentences in this game are a money complaint and a homecoming promise, because those were the twin engines of the correspondence from the first $8 forward. The teasing formality of 'the Paymaster,' capitalized like a personage of state, is Alexander's last piece of army comedy. He signed, was paid, and went home to the tea table; the ledger, and the letters, close together."),

dict(qid='a5-nation-2', act=5, theme='nation', source=('gap', 'V-nation', 0), recip='to Alexander', date_str='January 11, 1865',
  stem='Frances hopes “the rebellion is in its death throes” — but is “afraid to allow myself to hope too strongly.” What had the war taught her about hope?',
  choices=['That the newspapers always exaggerated Union victories', 'Discipline — four years of false dawns had made hope itself something to ration', 'That the Confederacy could never be beaten', 'That peace negotiations were already complete'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “I have thought so many times before” — she is citing her own history of burned hopes: 1862's spring, 1863's July, every ‘war ending soon’ that didn't. By 1865 the home front had learned to hold good news at arm's length.",
  whyWrong={'0': "She DID distrust the papers ('not the least reliance,' she once wrote) — but this sentence audits her own heart, not the press.",
            '3': "Negotiation was in the air — the Hampton Roads conference came three weeks later — and produced nothing. Her caution outperformed the rumor mill."},
  expansion="Chart the North's false dawns as Frances lived them: Richmond nearly taken (spring 1862 — then the Seven Days); Antietam's 'victory' (which cost her Henry); Gettysburg and Vicksburg ending it by autumn (then Chickamauga, and 1864's bloodiest year still ahead); Atlanta settling it (then Franklin's carnage in November). Civilian morale ran on this saw-tooth for four years, and its survivors developed exactly the instrument this sentence displays: hope held under discipline, optimism treated as a risk to be managed. She was, as it happens, RIGHT this time — Sherman had Savannah, Hood's army was wrecked, Fort Fisher fell four days after she wrote, and Appomattox came inside ninety days — but the caution wasn't error; it was calibration, the rational posture of someone who had paid full price for premature belief. Historians reading home-front letters treat sentences like this as the war's emotional actuarial tables: what hope cost, and what the experienced learned to pay for it.",
  context="Here is Frances's mind at full precision — the same letter that garrisons her streets and confesses she'd 'numbered' Alexander with the dead also runs this perfect self-audit: hope proposed, hope's track record reviewed, hope rationed. It's the maternal counterpart to her sons' battlefield fatalism: they managed fear with Providence, she managed it with epistemology — what may I safely believe, on what evidence, at what risk to my ability to keep functioning? Four years of widowed farm-keeping, one son unburied, three in uniform: her caution was load-bearing. Reading her beside Alexander's June valediction ('now by the providence of God…') completes the family's war: his sentence celebrates what hers didn't dare — and hers explains what his cost. The rebellion WAS in its death throes; she had simply earned the right not to say so without a hedge."),

dict(qid='a5-family-2', act=5, theme='family', source=('gap', 'V-family', 0), recip='to his mother', date_str='June 16, 1865',
  stem='Alexander explains his “long silence”: he’s been holding his letter, waiting for one particular piece of news. What news?',
  choices=['The result of the peace negotiations', 'Word of when the regiment would go home — the only headline left that mattered', 'His promotion to lieutenant', 'News of James’s regiment in Savannah'],
  answer=1,
  whyRight="That's the truth of it. “Waiting to see if there would not soon be some news of our going home” — after Appomattox, the war's correspondence ran on exactly one question, asked in every tent in every camp: when?",
  whyWrong={'0': "The armies' war was over — Johnston had surrendered in April. The negotiation that concerned a sergeant in June was between his regiment and a demobilization schedule.",
            '3': "The brothers did track each other to the end — but his held pen was waiting on HIS orders, the only news he could give her that counted."},
  expansion="Between Appomattox (April 9) and each regiment's muster-out stretched a season with no name — the war over, the army intact, a million men writing home variations of one sentence: no news yet of our going home. Demobilization ran by schedule and category (long-service regiments first), and the schedule was opaque from inside a camp, so rumor did its usual work at double speed; soldiers' letters of May–July 1865 are a genre of managed impatience, letters deliberately HELD (as Alexander holds this one) in hope of enclosing the only fact that mattered. Notice what the delay did to the correspondence itself: after four years of letters thick with battles, prices, politics, and sickness, the content thinned to logistics and longing — the war's great written conversation audibly winding down as its reason dissolved. The Hubbell collection's final pages are exactly this: short letters about schedules, money, and reunion, the archive documenting its own approaching silence.",
  context="'You have doubtless wondered at my long silence' — the apology every Hubbell letter-opener dreaded, deployed one last time for the happiest possible reason: he'd been saving the envelope for the word HOME. This is the June 16 letter's third appearance in this act (dissolution, hunger, and now homecoming — the war's whole ending in one page), and its family beat is the sweetest: after 272 letters of managing distance, the correspondence's final job is to schedule its own obsolescence. Six weeks later he's at Ogdensburgh promising the rest 'at the tea table,' and the record closes. One shadow for the reader who knows the whole story: the same going-home season that ends Alexander's letters is the one that takes James, en route from Savannah in October. The family got its reunion minus one — which is why the finale's words are what they are: two came home."),
]
